WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:05.020
Speaker 1: It's great to be joined today by
Professor Michael Sandel, professor of political

00:00:05.020 --> 00:00:12.660
philosophy at Harvard University and also
author of the now in paperback book, The Tyranny

00:00:12.660 --> 00:00:13.720
of Merit.

00:00:13.720 --> 00:00:15.750
Can we find the Common Good?

00:00:15.750 --> 00:00:16.750
It's so great to have you on.

00:00:16.750 --> 00:00:17.750
I appreciate your time.

00:00:17.750 --> 00:00:19.500
Speaker 4: Good to be with you, David.

00:00:19.500 --> 00:00:26.619
Speaker 1: So to maybe start talking a little
bit about the topic of of merit, there's often

00:00:26.619 --> 00:00:29.060
sort of like two layers to this discussion.

00:00:29.060 --> 00:00:36.780
And one is a focus on an argument as to whether
we or a particular country or society or economy

00:00:36.780 --> 00:00:38.569
have a real meritocracy.

00:00:38.569 --> 00:00:40.239
That's one piece of the conversation.

00:00:40.239 --> 00:00:45.800
The second part which is less discussed, but
arguably just as important, if we really did

00:00:45.800 --> 00:00:49.350
have a pure meritocracy, would that actually
be a good thing?

00:00:49.350 --> 00:00:50.450
What would be the concerns?

00:00:50.450 --> 00:00:56.370
What would be the problems if it was really
that everybody got what they deserved?

00:00:56.370 --> 00:00:59.100
From whatever sort of perspective we want
to approach that question.

00:00:59.100 --> 00:01:05.710
So can can we maybe start with how we can
combine these two questions and enter the

00:01:05.710 --> 00:01:08.259
kind of philosophy that you lay out in the
book?

00:01:08.259 --> 00:01:10.079
Speaker 4: Yes.

00:01:10.079 --> 00:01:14.049
And I do address both questions, and you're
right to distinguish them.

00:01:14.049 --> 00:01:15.870
It's very important.

00:01:15.870 --> 00:01:21.820
The first problem with meritocracy is that
we don't live up to the meritocratic principles

00:01:21.820 --> 00:01:23.700
we profess.

00:01:23.700 --> 00:01:30.259
In Ivy League universities, for example, there
are more students from the top one percent

00:01:30.259 --> 00:01:37.229
than there are from students and families
in the bottom half of the income scale combined.

00:01:37.229 --> 00:01:41.259
If you if you come from an affluent family.

00:01:41.259 --> 00:01:50.579
Top the top quarter, you're 70 percent of
the students who attend the 100 selected 100

00:01:50.579 --> 00:01:57.299
plus most selective colleges and universities,
70 percent come from the top quartile, well-off

00:01:57.299 --> 00:01:58.979
families.

00:01:58.979 --> 00:02:03.350
And you know, how many come from the from
the bottom quartile, from low income families.

00:02:03.350 --> 00:02:05.310
Speaker 1: It must be incredibly small.

00:02:05.310 --> 00:02:07.490
Speaker 4: It's three percent.

00:02:07.490 --> 00:02:08.490
Right.

00:02:08.490 --> 00:02:14.250
And now so we don't live up to the meritocratic
principles we profess, it isn't true, as the

00:02:14.250 --> 00:02:21.310
slogan we hear from politicians tells us that
you can make it if you cry, that everyone

00:02:21.310 --> 00:02:23.780
really has an equal chance to succeed.

00:02:23.780 --> 00:02:27.080
So that's one problem with meritocracy.

00:02:27.080 --> 00:02:28.280
We haven't realized it.

00:02:28.280 --> 00:02:34.310
We don't chances are not truly equal, but
there's a deeper problem, which is that the

00:02:34.310 --> 00:02:37.170
ideal is flawed.

00:02:37.170 --> 00:02:39.800
And this is really the heart of my argument.

00:02:39.800 --> 00:02:46.920
In the tyranny of merit, meritocracy has a
dark side and the dark side is that it is

00:02:46.920 --> 00:02:50.510
corrosive of the common good.

00:02:50.510 --> 00:02:54.850
And let me see if I can explain why, because
this is a counterintuitive idea, we normally

00:02:54.850 --> 00:03:00.810
think that if only we could perfect the meritocracy,
then we would have a just society.

00:03:00.810 --> 00:03:01.810
Right.

00:03:01.810 --> 00:03:06.700
But think about it, if it were really true.

00:03:06.700 --> 00:03:14.010
That everyone's place in society and in the
income scale were the result of their own

00:03:14.010 --> 00:03:21.820
doing such that they could say, I've earned
it, I deserve my place, then.

00:03:21.820 --> 00:03:27.500
Those who landed on top would believe their
success was their undoing, the measure of

00:03:27.500 --> 00:03:36.110
their merit, and they would look upon those
who struggle as deserving their fate as well.

00:03:36.110 --> 00:03:43.900
In fact, the person who coined the term meritocracy,
he was a British sociologist, Michael Young.

00:03:43.900 --> 00:03:52.370
In 1958, he coined it, The rise of the Meritocracy
was his book as a dystopian scenario.

00:03:52.370 --> 00:03:58.910
Because he glimpsed this, he glimpsed the
hubris that a meritocracy generates among

00:03:58.910 --> 00:04:06.800
the successful and the humiliation and resentment
it generates among those left behind.

00:04:06.800 --> 00:04:12.830
Now, you asked me, David, how do I bring these
two arguments together in the book?

00:04:12.830 --> 00:04:16.190
We've got, in a way, the worst of both worlds.

00:04:16.190 --> 00:04:25.402
We don't have a true meritocracy because opportunities
are not truly equal, and yet those who have

00:04:25.402 --> 00:04:31.510
landed on top, especially in recent decades,
have come to believe that their success is

00:04:31.510 --> 00:04:37.980
their own doing and that they therefore deserve
the full measure of the benefits that a market

00:04:37.980 --> 00:04:39.880
society showers upon them.

00:04:39.880 --> 00:04:43.040
So we have the hubris among the successful.

00:04:43.040 --> 00:04:49.201
We have the humiliation and the brewing resentment,
understandable resentment among those who

00:04:49.201 --> 00:04:54.440
struggle even without a genuine meritocracy.

00:04:54.440 --> 00:05:02.920
And and I think that the anger and resentment
among many working people, that elite, meritocratic,

00:05:02.920 --> 00:05:06.810
elite, credentialed elites look down on them.

00:05:06.810 --> 00:05:14.260
That set of resentments and anger, those grievances
have gave rise to Trump, Donald Trump, and

00:05:14.260 --> 00:05:21.380
they persist because the Democratic Party
really has become the party of meritocratic

00:05:21.380 --> 00:05:29.760
credentialed elites and has lost contact with
the working class, which once constituted

00:05:29.760 --> 00:05:31.830
its base going back to the New Deal.

00:05:31.830 --> 00:05:33.560
Speaker 1: That's that's very interesting.

00:05:33.560 --> 00:05:38.460
And there's a lot of aspects there that that
we could dig more into because you mentioned

00:05:38.460 --> 00:05:39.940
political party.

00:05:39.940 --> 00:05:43.310
It seems that one of the I don't even know
that it's a side effect.

00:05:43.310 --> 00:05:47.680
I mean, it's really a direct consequence of
the idea that everybody sort of has what they've

00:05:47.680 --> 00:05:51.360
earned and what they've deserved with equal
opportunity.

00:05:51.360 --> 00:05:57.860
It seems like politically it's a recipe to
justify doing very little to help those whose

00:05:57.860 --> 00:06:03.730
position is is lesser or less good, because
after all, it's a direct result merely of

00:06:03.730 --> 00:06:05.410
their actions that they are there.

00:06:05.410 --> 00:06:07.920
And in fact, it's this isn't theoretical.

00:06:07.920 --> 00:06:13.040
Some of the primary arguments made to cut
welfare programs against the so-called enhanced

00:06:13.040 --> 00:06:18.310
unemployment during the pandemic that we've
seen against all of these things is that it's

00:06:18.310 --> 00:06:22.340
some form of laziness or simply choosing one's
circumstances.

00:06:22.340 --> 00:06:24.210
Why would we reward people for that?

00:06:24.210 --> 00:06:26.140
It has a very direct line to policy.

00:06:26.140 --> 00:06:27.610
Speaker 4: It does.

00:06:27.610 --> 00:06:33.230
And we hear this argument all the time in
debates about welfare and other forms of income

00:06:33.230 --> 00:06:34.340
support.

00:06:34.340 --> 00:06:42.670
What's interesting is the way troubling, but
also important to come to terms with the way

00:06:42.670 --> 00:06:50.140
the Democratic Party has migrated from being
a party that speaks for the middle class and

00:06:50.140 --> 00:06:58.180
the working class against the privileged to
being really the embodiment of credentialled,

00:06:58.180 --> 00:07:01.949
well credentialed elites and the professional
classes.

00:07:01.949 --> 00:07:08.990
And this happened really over the last four
decades as the Democratic Party, along with

00:07:08.990 --> 00:07:17.680
Republicans, embraced a kind of market driven,
finance driven version of globalization and

00:07:17.680 --> 00:07:23.560
education, admonishing people to improve themselves
through higher education.

00:07:23.560 --> 00:07:30.290
That's been a big part of the message, especially
of the Democratic Party in the face of rising

00:07:30.290 --> 00:07:38.020
inequality and wage stagnation and job loss
over the last four decades.

00:07:38.020 --> 00:07:44.970
What we've heard from politicians, mainstream
politicians, is not an attempt to deal directly

00:07:44.970 --> 00:07:48.530
with inequalities of income and wealth and
power.

00:07:48.530 --> 00:07:52.080
They've offered a kind of meritocratic workaround.

00:07:52.080 --> 00:07:57.940
They've they've offered individual upward
mobility through higher education.

00:07:57.940 --> 00:08:02.320
If you want to compete and win in the global
economy, go to college.

00:08:02.320 --> 00:08:05.210
What you earn will depend on what you learn.

00:08:05.210 --> 00:08:06.410
That was Bill Clinton.

00:08:06.410 --> 00:08:09.000
You can make it if you try.

00:08:09.000 --> 00:08:11.330
That was Barack Obama.

00:08:11.330 --> 00:08:16.740
But what they miss now, it teams inspiring
to encourage everybody to go to college or

00:08:16.740 --> 00:08:22.320
university and increasing access, broadening
access to higher education.

00:08:22.320 --> 00:08:23.320
That's a good thing.

00:08:23.320 --> 00:08:27.010
I'd be the last person to argue against that.

00:08:27.010 --> 00:08:34.599
But as a response, or is the primary response
to the rising inequality of the globalization

00:08:34.599 --> 00:08:42.560
decades and is a response to the wage stagnation
and job losses experienced by many working

00:08:42.560 --> 00:08:44.079
people?

00:08:44.079 --> 00:08:50.779
That is a pale offer because those who pass
it contains an implicit insult.

00:08:50.779 --> 00:08:55.310
If you think about it right, improve your
condition.

00:08:55.310 --> 00:09:00.079
You will surmount wage stagnation and job
loss and inequality.

00:09:00.079 --> 00:09:03.320
The problem is that you aren't well enough
educated.

00:09:03.320 --> 00:09:08.399
The problem is not that we embrace policies
that created this widening gap.

00:09:08.399 --> 00:09:10.899
It's that you are not well enough educated.

00:09:10.899 --> 00:09:19.619
So that was the insult implicit in the seemingly
inspiring admonition to go get a better education.

00:09:19.619 --> 00:09:28.149
And the result was rising resentment among
people who felt that their concerns were not

00:09:28.149 --> 00:09:29.220
being taken seriously.

00:09:29.220 --> 00:09:31.930
Speaker 1: Yeah, there's a couple of interesting
things there.

00:09:31.930 --> 00:09:37.000
I mean, so we've seen a story that I first
remember hearing it.

00:09:37.000 --> 00:09:41.690
I think it was with Rick, the senator from
Pennsylvania, Rick Santorum, about maybe 20

00:09:41.690 --> 00:09:48.240
years ago, where he started to say, you know,
we're we're education is liberal indoctrination.

00:09:48.240 --> 00:09:52.689
We're never going to have those people on
our side because these mechanisms of colleges

00:09:52.689 --> 00:09:57.149
and universities, they're just going to indoctrinate
people into left wing sort of points of view.

00:09:57.149 --> 00:10:03.029
The left, on the other hand, has talked about
let's get people college educations, let's

00:10:03.029 --> 00:10:06.980
look at free college, let's look at making
it cheaper, more accessible, et cetera.

00:10:06.980 --> 00:10:08.689
And there's a couple of effects of that.

00:10:08.689 --> 00:10:13.999
One is that it's saying if and until you do
that, you will be less than the people that

00:10:13.999 --> 00:10:15.139
have done that.

00:10:15.139 --> 00:10:20.510
But also, isn't there some degree to which
if everybody went ahead and did that, doesn't

00:10:20.510 --> 00:10:26.720
it sort of zero out and not even actually
solve the problems of inequality that it claims

00:10:26.720 --> 00:10:27.990
to resolve?

00:10:27.990 --> 00:10:29.660
Speaker 4: You're right.

00:10:29.660 --> 00:10:32.040
It does not solve the problem of inequality.

00:10:32.040 --> 00:10:40.050
I think it's desirable for its own sake to
make college free and or at least as accessible

00:10:40.050 --> 00:10:41.319
as possible.

00:10:41.319 --> 00:10:42.319
Yes.

00:10:42.319 --> 00:10:49.390
People from every background that are good
in and of itself and I'm all in favor of it.

00:10:49.390 --> 00:10:56.749
But the question is whether that is an adequate
response to the inequality and wage stagnation

00:10:56.749 --> 00:10:58.420
of recent decades.

00:10:58.420 --> 00:11:01.470
And I say it isn't.

00:11:01.470 --> 00:11:07.619
First of all, those of us who spend our days
in the company of credential of the credentialled

00:11:07.619 --> 00:11:11.360
can easily forget a simple fact.

00:11:11.360 --> 00:11:16.019
Most Americans do not have a four year college
degree.

00:11:16.019 --> 00:11:17.750
Nearly two thirds do not.

00:11:17.750 --> 00:11:18.750
Right.

00:11:18.750 --> 00:11:27.240
So it's folly to create an economy that sets
is a necessary condition of dignified work

00:11:27.240 --> 00:11:32.269
and a decent life, a college degree that most
people don't have.

00:11:32.269 --> 00:11:41.459
It also this way of thinking is too, too blinkered
to narrow an approach to inequality because

00:11:41.459 --> 00:11:48.740
it leaves in place the steep hierarchy of
prestige and esteem associated with people

00:11:48.740 --> 00:11:51.129
who who are well credentialed.

00:11:51.129 --> 00:11:52.129
Right.

00:11:52.129 --> 00:11:55.139
And it implicitly disparages those who aren't.

00:11:55.139 --> 00:11:59.129
And this is embodied in policy and in funding.

00:11:59.129 --> 00:12:05.839
An economist at the Brookings Institute Figured
Institution figured out that we spend about

00:12:05.839 --> 00:12:13.970
one hundred and sixty four billion dollars
a year helping people go to college.

00:12:13.970 --> 00:12:20.160
And how much do we spend supporting people
who go in for vocational and technical training

00:12:20.160 --> 00:12:22.429
in community colleges?

00:12:22.429 --> 00:12:28.439
At the federal level, about one billion hundred
and sixty two to one.

00:12:28.439 --> 00:12:37.170
Now there is there's something wrong with
that mode of funding for those forms of learning,

00:12:37.170 --> 00:12:43.790
including community colleges and technical
and vocational training that many people rely

00:12:43.790 --> 00:12:47.220
on to prepare themselves for the world work.

00:12:47.220 --> 00:12:55.989
But this this imbalance reflects this broader
skewing of our values in the direction of

00:12:55.989 --> 00:13:03.899
valorizing people who are well credentialed
and implicitly disparaging those who have

00:13:03.899 --> 00:13:05.499
not been to university.

00:13:05.499 --> 00:13:08.569
And this is showing up in the way people are
voting.

00:13:08.569 --> 00:13:10.660
Trump managed.

00:13:10.660 --> 00:13:20.470
To tap into the grievance against elites that
many working people felt, and you can look

00:13:20.470 --> 00:13:27.889
at the voting behavior in the last few decades
as a college, the college educated vote has

00:13:27.889 --> 00:13:35.529
gone increasingly for Democrats and the vote
among those who haven't been to college goes

00:13:35.529 --> 00:13:42.279
increasingly for Republicans and especially
Republicans like Trump of an authoritarian

00:13:42.279 --> 00:13:46.799
populist bent who channel the anger in the
sense of grievance.

00:13:46.799 --> 00:13:52.949
And what's interesting is a similar pattern
is unfolding in Britain and in France and

00:13:52.949 --> 00:14:03.199
in other countries that have embraced globalization,
emphasize the meritocratic solution to inequality

00:14:03.199 --> 00:14:05.720
and have alienated center left parties.

00:14:05.720 --> 00:14:09.759
Speaker 1: Now, there are some who would look
at that phenomenon that you identify as the

00:14:09.759 --> 00:14:14.059
college educated voter, voting more for Democrats,
voting more for center left and left wing

00:14:14.059 --> 00:14:19.850
parties in the UK and say, well, this is there's
a very simple, intuitive explanation for that.

00:14:19.850 --> 00:14:25.059
As you become more educated and you learn
more about the world, you realize it's better

00:14:25.059 --> 00:14:26.059
for everybody.

00:14:26.059 --> 00:14:27.949
It's better for the country to vote for these
people.

00:14:27.949 --> 00:14:29.420
You're doing an altruistic thing.

00:14:29.420 --> 00:14:32.160
You're doing a rational thing, and we're all
better off for it.

00:14:32.160 --> 00:14:33.619
Speaker 4: Right.

00:14:33.619 --> 00:14:41.139
And that in itself, I think, is a hubristic
analysis of the situation, because, in fact,

00:14:41.139 --> 00:14:50.309
the policies, the kind of finance driven globalization
or neoliberal policies embraced by center

00:14:50.309 --> 00:14:54.489
right, center left parties, really center
left and center right.

00:14:54.489 --> 00:14:59.610
But politically, the toll has been taken upon
the Democratic Party in the U.S., the Labor

00:14:59.610 --> 00:15:04.240
Party in Britain, the Socialist Party in France.

00:15:04.240 --> 00:15:12.999
These policies have failed in the 2008 financial
crisis was the most dramatic illustration

00:15:12.999 --> 00:15:18.220
of how a kind of finance driven globalization
project fails.

00:15:18.220 --> 00:15:27.089
The recession that followed, which fell very
heavily on low and middle income Americans,

00:15:27.089 --> 00:15:29.309
demonstrated demonstrated further.

00:15:29.309 --> 00:15:37.250
The failure and then to add insult to injury
is the meritocratic attempt to say that the

00:15:37.250 --> 00:15:44.529
solution to to inequality and wage stagnation
is not to reconsider the policies that we

00:15:44.529 --> 00:15:48.319
promoted and enacted for 30 or 40 years.

00:15:48.319 --> 00:15:50.209
It simply for you to improve yourselves.

00:15:50.209 --> 00:15:53.290
There is something deeply disparaging about
that.

00:15:53.290 --> 00:16:01.160
So I think that what I'm proposing for the
Democratic Party is a shift to focus less

00:16:01.160 --> 00:16:06.759
on arming people for meritocratic competition
and to focus more on the dignity of work to

00:16:06.759 --> 00:16:14.879
ask what would it take to make life better
for everyone, regardless of of the university

00:16:14.879 --> 00:16:16.600
credentials they may have.

00:16:16.600 --> 00:16:18.230
And we could explore.

00:16:18.230 --> 00:16:23.589
And there will be debates about what counts,
what policies actually support and affirm

00:16:23.589 --> 00:16:24.620
the dignity of work.

00:16:24.620 --> 00:16:27.100
But I think that should be the starting point.

00:16:27.100 --> 00:16:32.359
Speaker 1: Last thing I want to touch on,
are there societies that we can look at either

00:16:32.359 --> 00:16:38.290
today or historically that have had maybe
what we would call a more balanced or productive

00:16:38.290 --> 00:16:43.660
or rational view on the kind of interaction
between merit and and equality that might

00:16:43.660 --> 00:16:46.439
be interesting as a model in some sense?

00:16:46.439 --> 00:16:49.740
Speaker 4: Well, in some ways.

00:16:49.740 --> 00:16:57.079
And this is not to engage in golden age nostalgia,
but the political coalitions that formed during

00:16:57.079 --> 00:17:04.639
the time of FDR and the New Deal were an example
of coalitions that did not depend on admonishing

00:17:04.639 --> 00:17:10.839
working people to improve themselves, but
instead asked how can we structurally reform

00:17:10.839 --> 00:17:19.150
the economy to strengthen collective bargaining,
to strengthen unions, to give working people

00:17:19.150 --> 00:17:25.669
more of a voice, more of a say, as well as
to be able to negotiate better working conditions?

00:17:25.669 --> 00:17:35.940
I think today, in some ways, Biden has begun
to depart from the meritocratic, technocratic,

00:17:35.940 --> 00:17:39.260
neoliberal orientation of his predecessors.

00:17:39.260 --> 00:17:47.460
And it's interesting to notice that Joe Biden
was the first Democratic nominee in 36 years.

00:17:47.460 --> 00:17:52.720
Without an Ivy League degree, Rice may have
opened him at least a little to a broader

00:17:52.720 --> 00:17:53.720
horizon.

00:17:53.720 --> 00:17:59.260
He speaks more actually about the dignity
of work than he does about meritocratic striving

00:17:59.260 --> 00:18:01.500
and meritocratic competition.

00:18:01.500 --> 00:18:06.770
There's also an interesting political figure
now looked at the German elections, the social

00:18:06.770 --> 00:18:10.659
Democratic candidate, Schultz.

00:18:10.659 --> 00:18:17.190
Has been trying to make this shift as well,
to focus, he's actually offered a critique

00:18:17.190 --> 00:18:23.380
of the single minded focus on merit and has
emphasized instead notions of respect and

00:18:23.380 --> 00:18:26.010
the dignity of work.

00:18:26.010 --> 00:18:30.310
And I think that this is a more promising
direction for the Democratic Party and for

00:18:30.310 --> 00:18:31.980
the center left parties generally.

00:18:31.980 --> 00:18:35.260
Speaker 1: I could not could not agree more.

00:18:35.260 --> 00:18:41.720
And much of what we've been talking about
today is in the just out in paperback book,

00:18:41.720 --> 00:18:43.380
The Tyranny of Merit.

00:18:43.380 --> 00:18:45.610
Can We Find the Common Good?

00:18:45.610 --> 00:18:50.470
We've been speaking with the book's author,
Professor Michael Sandel from Harvard University.

00:18:50.470 --> 00:18:52.620
So appreciate your time and your insights
today.

00:18:52.620 --> 00:18:53.830
Speaker 4: Thank you, David.

